    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d540">

                <pb facs="1053" n="1021" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v13p1/pg1021"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">396.1 BE/2–654: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Secretary of State, at
                            Berlin</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d540-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top
                            secret<lb/>priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-02-06T17:26:00-05:00">February 6, 1954—5:26
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Tedul 27. Re Dulte 40.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d540-f2"/> Appreciate French
                    pressures for negotiated Indochina settlement. We wonder whether preliminary
                    private conversations between French and British and/or Russians might not have
                    occurred. If some formula envisaging eventual negotiations is unavoidable, we
                    hope it will conform as closely as possible to language penultimate paragraph
                        Dulte 35.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d540-f3"/>
                    You, of course, will know best whether to recall that French associated
                    themselves at UNGA last August with view that
                    favorable developments at Korean political conference should precede discussion
                    of other Asian questions with states concerned with those questions. If that
                    position is abandoned and we appear to be suing for negotiated peace, Communists
                    may well conclude situation so desperate in Indochina they need only stand firm
                    to win full victory. Negotiations in such circumstances not likely produce
                    agreement but could further sap French will to resist in Indochina.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d540-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d540-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted by Robert D. Murphy, Deputy Under
                    Secretary of State, and David H. Popper, Deputy Director of
                    the Office of United Nations Political Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d540-f2" n="2" xml:id="d540-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram Dulte 40 from Berlin, Feb. 6, reported
                    on a French draft regarding a five-power conference and Indochina negotiations,
                    which had been circulated that day at a meeting of the Western Foreign
                    Ministers. (396.1 BE/2–654) For documentation on this subject, see <ref
                        target="frus1952-54v07p1">volume <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                    >vii</hi></ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d540-f3" n="3" xml:id="d540-f3-content">
                <p>Telegram Dulte 35 from Berlin, Feb. 4,
                    contained the text of a U.S. draft on the question of a five-power conference,
                    transmitted to the French and British Delegations that day. (396.1 BE/2–454) For
                    documentation on this subject, see <ref target="frus1952-54v07p1">volume <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">vii</hi></ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
